WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.790
Film Courage: At what moment did you say “I’m
really going to do this!”

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:07.040
Because we all love to think of these awesome
ideas and then they last what, maybe a day,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.480
a week, whatever?

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.650
That is essentially what the book (NFA) is
about, actually doing something, not just

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.650
talking about it.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.970
Jeff Leisawitz: [Laughs] Right…One of my
favorite quotes is “Between concept and

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.520
execution, there is a land of shadow!”

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.650
Film Courage: [Laughs] That’s good!

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:31.480
Jeff Leisawitz: There is another one I love
which is “When all is said and done, there

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.220
is generally a lot more said than done.”

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.490
Film Courage: Very true…and maybe…different
in the Seattle area than it is in Los Angeles

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.190
but…LA can be known for this.

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.670
Jeff Leisawitz: So when did I really gear
into this?

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:51.980
I think it was sort of in that moment or time
period when I realized that this was really

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.260
coming from a deep place within me.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:03.250
That both the creative side and the empowerment
and inspirational side.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:04.580
These things came together in the book.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.950
And the book is one thing and we’re getting
that out and whatever (people are digging

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.530
it) but I’m also doing lots of workshops.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:20.120
And this for me is actually more powerful
because, you know, I already wrote the book.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.189
Hopefully people will read it and that’s
great.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:28.400
But interacting with groups of people, whether
online or in live situations really gives

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.160
me a chance to connect and obviously there
is a bit more to it than reading a book, like

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.000
there are humans involved.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.090
Film Courage: The book (NFA) has a lot of
humor in it and I enjoyed the drawings as

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.090
well.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.690
You have one illustration that is fairly close
to the beginning of the book where there is

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:51.530
a guy sitting on his couch watching television,
kind of slumped down and depressed with maybe

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.020
a beer on the table next to him.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.180
I find it interesting because this is the
acceptable form of recreation and we’ve

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.920
all probably fallen prey to it (it’s easy
to go there).

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.840
Why do you think so many people reach for…not
just a beer or whatever (we don’t have to

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.670
talk about addiction or anything) but that
safe place on the couch?

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.040
Why is this so commonplace?

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.960
Jeff Leisawitz: Because it’s easy.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.180
[Laughs] Right?

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:20.030
I mean it’s easy to lay there, drink a beer
and click around or eat your ice cream or

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.340
whatever you’re into.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.120
It takes energy to do anything.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:33.510
Whether you are going to write your book or
make your film or write a song or any of those

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.420
creative things take energy.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.590
But here’s the difference, at the end of
the day when you’re done watching your TV

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.620
show…okay…fine.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.290
Maybe it was good, maybe it s*cked?

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.090
That’s the end of it.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.150
When you create something, that energy comes
back to you.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.420
It doesn’t come back to you with the TV
show, generally.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.880
Film Courage: That’s a good point.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.920
Jeff Leisawitz: So the more energy you put
in, the more energy you have, if it’s something

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.420
that you genuinely, absolutely love.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.959
Film Courage: Now you talk about this in the
book and forgive me if I’m getting too personal

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:10.660
with it…but you said during the time when
you were going to attend high school or leaving

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.550
high school to attend college, that you had
a lot of people around you that you were close

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.950
to pass away?

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.760
Jeff Leisawitz: Yes.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:23.870
Film Courage: Was this part of that NFA mindset
that you adopted because you witnessed that

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.989
people probably had best intentions and plans.

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:33.849
Jeff Leisawitz: It was definitely a very tough
time for me coming out of high school and

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:40.190
you know “the world is in front of us”
kind of thing and suddenly seeing “Wow.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:46.520
This thing can end and has for several of
my friends.”

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:57.900
It was a huge shock and a deep introspection
to just know that time is always ticking.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.000
We don’t know if we’re going to get another
50 years or 10 minutes, but there is always

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.700
a limit to this life, as far as we know.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.160
So what are you going to do with it?

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.840
And for me (you’re right) after I got out
of that funk it was like “Okay, let’s

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.469
do something meaningful with life.

00:04:20.469 --> 00:04:22.099
It’s a gift.”

00:04:22.099 --> 00:04:26.939
Film Courage: Do you remember what got you
out of that funk?

00:04:26.939 --> 00:04:35.849
Jeff Leisawitz: It might have been meeting
the greatest guitar player I’ve ever played

00:04:35.849 --> 00:04:39.620
with, a guy called Mike Schmidt in college.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:46.689
I met this guy…lot of people play guitar
and I played music with people back then.

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:55.440
But Mike was an extraordinary musician, like
ultra level.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.669
And for some reason he wanted to play with
me in a band.

00:05:00.669 --> 00:05:04.120
He taught me a whole lot.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.699
Not just about music but about life through
music.

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:14.339
So the story that I remember that was really
so huge for me was at that time I would learn

00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:18.319
a song and play it (do a cover song or write
a song).

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:25.150
I went to get together with Mike and I’m
like “Mike, what song do you want to play?”

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.919
And he’s like “I don’t want to play
a song.”

00:05:27.919 --> 00:05:29.780
And I’m like “What do you want to do?”

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.379
and he’s like “Just play.

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:36.289
Just play.”

00:05:36.289 --> 00:05:43.680
And I start playing the bass and Mike starts
carving music out of out of my semi-chaotic,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.289
erratic bass line and it was beautiful!

00:05:48.289 --> 00:05:53.460
And what I got out of that was how music was
completely open and free, which is similar

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.110
to life, right?

00:05:55.110 --> 00:06:00.120
The more structured you are, there is great
stuff in that.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.559
But if you can be free and loose with your
plan, magic can happen.

00:06:05.559 --> 00:06:07.770
It’s like a Grateful Dead song.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.249
You ever listen to the Dead?

00:06:09.249 --> 00:06:11.919
Film Courage: Well I grew up in a town that
worshipped the Grateful Dead.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:15.699
I grew up in Palo Also, so yeah, every year
there was a huge Dead concert/festival essentially.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:18.710
[Laughs]
Jeff Leisawitz: Right…so the Dead sort of

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:25.889
jam every night (or used to) and some nights
it was just a disaster!

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:30.960
It was a sonic mess that you just never would
think…what the hell?

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.619
Then…other nights it was music that was
just so heavenly and divine.

00:06:36.619 --> 00:06:40.960
Like how can humans make something so beautiful?

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.669
They have to step into that chaos.

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:47.409
They have to step into the possibility and
just see how it goes.

00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:50.639
That’s kind of what I got from Mike back
then.

